WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.520
Model Context Protocol, often shortened to MCP,

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.165
is a protocol pattern for connecting AI applications to external tools, data, resources,

00:00:08.165 --> 00:00:09.180
and services.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.435
The core idea is simple:

00:00:10.435 --> 00:00:13.990
an AI system is more useful when it can work with relevant context and approved

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.270
capabilities outside the model itself.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:20.920
Instead of every AI application inventing a different integration style, MCP gives hosts,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.040
clients, and servers a more consistent way to describe and use external capabilities.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.720
A model is the component that generates or reasons over text and other inputs.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.840
An AI application is the product or environment that wraps the model for users.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.170
An agent is an application pattern where the system may plan, select tools,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.875
and take steps toward a task.

00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:40.475
An integration connects the application to another system.

00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:45.170
An MCP connection is one structured way to expose those integrations so the AI application

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:46.520
can discover resources,

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.560
call tools, or use prompt templates under defined rules.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.775
MCP is often compared to a standard integration layer for AI applications because it

00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:57.440
separates the AI experience from the mechanics of each external system.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.575
A developer tool, a documentation source, a ticketing platform, a file system, a database,

00:01:02.575 --> 00:01:06.430
or an internal API can be exposed through a server with capabilities the host can

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:07.260
understand.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.160
That does not mean every capability is automatically safe or appropriate.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.360
It means the integration surface can be made more consistent and governable.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.600
Developers care about MCP because it can reduce repeated integration work.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.110
Security teams care because it makes data flow, permissions,

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.140
and tool use easier to inventory and review.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.750
Operations teams care because connected tools need ownership, logging, reliability,

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.160
and incident response.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.615
Product teams care because MCP can make AI features more useful without hard-coding every

00:01:32.615 --> 00:01:33.355
connection.

00:01:33.355 --> 00:01:36.295
The most important habit is to understand MCP as architecture,

00:01:36.295 --> 00:01:37.800
not merely as a plugin feature.
